  "contentPlaintext": "<bold>Contributing Guide</bold>\nThank you for taking the time to contribute to our open-source project. This document provides the guidelines, technical workflows, and quality standards required to propose code updates or documentation improvements.\n<bold>1. Table of Contents</bold>\n1. Table of Contents\n2. Code of Conduct Alignment\n3. Issue Tracking & Triaging Lifecycle\n3.1 Reporting Found System Defects\n3.2 Proposing New System Capabilities\n4. Fork-and-Pull Development Workflow\n4.1 Core Code Branching Conventions\n4.2 Local Test Verification Rules\n5. Pull Request Submission Checklist\n<bold>2. Code of Conduct Alignment</bold>\nBy participating in this repository as a contributor, you agree to uphold our community standards. Please review the full Code of Conduct documentation inside this workspace directory before opening issues or submitting code modifications.\n<bold>3. Issue Tracking & Triaging Lifecycle</bold>\n<bold>3.1 Reporting Found System Defects</bold>\nIf you isolate a runtime bug or an unhandled processing exception, submit a ticket to the triage column using our structured layout form:\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Clear Description:</textStyle></bold> Detail the precise steps needed to replicate the error on clean container environments.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Console Log Dumps:</textStyle></bold> Include raw stack traces, terminal printouts, and client configuration data.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Environment Specs:</textStyle></bold> Name your local operating system version, browser family, and runtime version profiles.\n<bold>3.2 Proposing New System Capabilities</bold>\nWe encourage community feature proposals. Before writing code, open an issue labeled <code>enhancement</code> or <code>help-wanted</code> to map out your feature vision with the active maintainer core team. This step prevents duplicate effort and ensures design choices match our system architecture.\n<bold>4. Fork-and-Pull Development Workflow</bold>\n<bold>4.1 Core Code Branching Conventions</bold>\nThe repository maintainers track contributions through a fork-and-pull pipeline. Name your local working branches after active issues using these clean naming rules:\nbugfix/issue-ticket-*   ➔ Patches fixing a confirmed system defect or security vulnerability.\nfeature/issue-ticket-*  ➔ Enhancements adding a new functional block or public API route.\ndocs/issue-ticket-*     ➔ Updates modifying setup files, guide markdown text, or inline references.\n<bold>4.2 Local Test Verification Rules</bold>\nYour code modifications must clear all validation parameters before moving into the review queue:\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Automated Linter Scans:</textStyle></bold> Run local code linting scripts to clean up formatting issues before staging your files.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Test Coverage Baselines:</textStyle></bold> Code changes must maintain an automated test coverage pass threshold of at least <bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">80%</textStyle></bold>.\n<bold>5. Pull Request Submission Checklist</bold>\nWhen your code changes are ready for core team evaluation, open a Pull Request (PR) and fill out the validation checklist template:\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Issue Linkage:</textStyle></bold> The PR description explicitly references the open tracking issue ID number.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Test Execution:</textStyle></bold> Local validation test suites run and clear successfully with zero terminal errors.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Documentation Check:</textStyle></bold> All public interface changes, configuration updates, and shell variable additions have matching markdown guides.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Trunk Integration:</textStyle></bold> The branch passes automated merge conflict checks against the <code>main</code> branch.",
  "blocks": []
